// TASK 3: Key Storage: HSM vs Software KMS
$task_contents[3] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Where a Key Lives</h2>

    <p>Once a key exists, it has to live somewhere, and where it lives determines how easily it can be stolen. A <strong>hardware security module</strong>, or HSM, is a physical, tamper resistant device purpose built to generate and store keys so that the raw key material never leaves the device.</p>

    <p>Think of an HSM as a bank vault built directly into the wall. A software KMS is closer to a very good safe sitting in an office. Both can protect what is inside, but the threat model, cost, and physical guarantees are different.</p>

    <p>HSMs are typically certified against <strong>FIPS 140</strong>, a US government standard series that defines security requirements for cryptographic hardware across multiple assurance levels. Software KMS platforms rely instead on operating system protections and process isolation, which is a weaker physical guarantee but far more flexible and cheaper to scale.</p>

