// TASK 4: Key Rotation Policies
$task_contents[4] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Why Keys Do Not Live Forever</h2>

    <p><strong>Key rotation</strong> is the process of replacing an active key with a new one on a regular schedule, even when there is no known compromise. The goal is to limit the blast radius: if a key is ever exposed, it should only have been valid for a limited window rather than the entire lifetime of a system.</p>

    <p>This mirrors changing the locks on a building periodically even without evidence of a break in. You cannot prove that nobody ever made a copy of the old key, so you simply limit how long any single key stays valid.</p>

    <p>Rotation only works safely if old ciphertext remains readable afterward. This is where <strong>key versioning</strong> comes in. Rather than deleting the old key immediately, the KMS keeps previous versions available for decryption while all new encryption operations use the newest version.</p>

    <!-- SVG: Key rotation with versioning timeline
         Teaching purpose: show the learner how old ciphertext remains
         decryptable under a previous key version after rotation, which
         they would otherwise misunderstand as rotation breaking access
         to old data. -->
    <div style="margin: 20px 0;">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 760 130" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Key rotation timeline with versioning">
