// TASK 5: Access Control to Key Material
$task_contents[5] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Who Gets to Touch a Key</h2>

    <p>Access control for keys applies the principle of <strong>least privilege</strong>, the idea that every role should receive only the permissions it actually needs to do its job, nothing more. Applied to key material, this means separating who can simply use a key from who can manage it entirely.</p>

    <p>Think of a valet key for a car. It can start the engine and drive the car, but it cannot open the trunk or the glovebox. The owner's full key can do everything. Most systems that interact with a key should only ever receive the valet version of that key's permissions.</p>

    <p>In practice this means separating permissions like encrypt, decrypt, rotate, export, and delete, and assigning them individually per role rather than granting broad administrative access to every service that happens to need encryption.</p>

    <h3>Example Role Permission Matrix</h3>
    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Role</th>
                <th>Encrypt / Decrypt</th>
                <th>Rotate</th>
                <th>Export</th>
                <th>Delete</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Application</td>
                <td>Yes</td>
                <td>No</td>
                <td>No</td>
                <td>No</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Admin</td>
                <td>Yes</td>
                <td>Yes</td>
                <td>No</td>
                <td>No</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Auditor</td>
                <td>No</td>
                <td>No</td>
                <td>No</td>
                <td>No</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="info-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> Why This Matters</h4>
        <p>Export permission should almost never be granted. If a key can be exported, its protection no longer depends on the KMS at all, because the raw key material can now leave the security boundary entirely.</p>
    </div>
</div>
