// TASK 6: Key Destruction
$task_contents[6] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>The Point of No Return</h2>

    <p>Destroying a key is the final stage of its lifecycle, and it is irreversible by design. Rather than trying to destroy every copy of the data a key protects, systems rely on <strong>cryptographic erasure</strong>, deleting the key itself so that the data it encrypted becomes permanently unreadable, even though the ciphertext still physically exists.</p>

    <p>This is the digital equivalent of shredding the only key to a lockbox instead of trying to destroy the lockbox itself. The box and its contents remain intact, but nothing inside can ever be retrieved again.</p>

    <p>Because deleting a key is catastrophic if triggered by mistake, most cloud KMS providers enforce a mandatory <strong>waiting period</strong>, typically between 7 and 30 days, between requesting deletion and the key actually being destroyed. During this window, the deletion can still be cancelled.</p>

    <div class="warning-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-exclamation-triangle"></i> Common Mistake</h4>
        <p>Treating key deletion the same as deleting an ordinary resource. Unlike most cloud resources, a deleted key cannot be recreated with the same identity, and every piece of data it protected becomes unrecoverable the moment it is gone.</p>
    </div>
</div>
HTML;

// TASK 7: Cloud KMS Architectures and Envelope Encryption
$task_contents[7] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>One Pattern, Three Providers</h2>

    <p>AWS KMS, Azure Key Vault, and HashiCorp Vault all appear different on the surface, with different APIs and terminology, but underneath they implement the same core pattern: <strong>envelope encryption</strong>. A key encryption key, or KEK, wraps a data encryption key, or DEK, and the DEK is what actually encrypts the bulk data.</p>

    <p>The KMS itself never touches the bulk data at all. It only ever wraps and unwraps small data encryption keys, which is exactly why cloud KMS calls stay fast and cheap even when the data being protected is enormous.</p>

